drm/msm/a6xx: stop the HFI interrupt taking polled acknowledges

The GMU reports the replies to HFI messages and to OOB requests through
bits of GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO, and the driver polls that register for them.
The HFI interrupt is only meant for the firmware fault bit, but
a6xx_hfi_irq() writes back everything it reads, so when it runs it also
clears a reply that its waiter has not seen yet. The waiter then times
out with the reply sitting in the queue, and the next message trips over
it:

  a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interrupt: Message HFI_H2F_MSG_GX_BW_PERF_VOTE
    id 282 timed out waiting for response
  a6xx_hfi_send_msg: Unexpected message id 282 on the response queue

The handler is not supposed to run at all outside a fault, but it does.
GMU2HOST_INTR_MASK reads 0 when the GMU powers up, everything unmasked,
and a6xx_gmu_resume() does not write it until the boot messages have been
exchanged. disable_irq() in the previous suspend is lazy, so the replies
to those messages raise the line. On the Galaxy Tab S8 Ultra (Adreno 730)
the handler was then entered 11 to 300 us after enable_irq(), which is
when a6xx_gmu_resume() sends its frequency vote.

Measured with a client that lets the GPU runtime suspend and then asks
for MSM_PARAM_TIMESTAMP, one GMU resume per query. In 800 resumes the
handler ran 112 times, found the message queue bit set 12 times, and the
log has exactly 12 timed out votes, each a one second stall. It needs the
CPUs to be allowed into their power-collapse idle state: with cpuidle
state1 disabled 1200 resumes had none.

Mask the HFI interrupts before the firmware starts, and only acknowledge
the bits the handler deals with. Either change alone gave 600 resumes
without a stall where the unchanged driver had 12 in 800; with the mask
written early the handler did not run once.

A compositor that reads the GPU timestamp every frame resumes the GMU
after each 66 ms of idle, so on a desktop this was a visible freeze every
few seconds. The OOB acknowledge bits are in the same register and
"Timeout waiting for GMU OOB set GPU_SET" preceded the hangcheck recovery
that took the Plasma session down.

Generated from Linux 7.2 with the stable RPMh stop fix (51fcee9d4140,
first in v7.2.6) applied; it applies unchanged to 7.2.8, which does not
have this fix. Not yet submitted upstream.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
@@ -59,12 +59,22 @@
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
 
+#define A6XX_HFI_IRQ_MASK \
+	(A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_CM3_FAULT)
+
 static irqreturn_t a6xx_hfi_irq(int irq, void *data)
 {
 	struct a6xx_gmu *gmu = data;
 	u32 status;
 
+	/*
+	 * The message queue and OOB acknowledge bits of this register are
+	 * polled by whoever is waiting for them. Only take the bits that are
+	 * handled here, or an acknowledge can be cleared before its waiter
+	 * has seen it.
+	 */
 	status = gmu_read(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO);
+	status &= A6XX_HFI_IRQ_MASK;
 	gmu_write(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_CLR, status);
 
 	if (status & A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_CM3_FAULT) {
@@ -1063,9 +1073,6 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
-#define A6XX_HFI_IRQ_MASK \
-	(A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_CM3_FAULT)
-
 #define A6XX_GMU_IRQ_MASK \
 	(A6XX_GMU_AO_HOST_INTERRUPT_STATUS_WDOG_BITE | \
 	 A6XX_GMU_AO_HOST_INTERRUPT_STATUS_HOST_AHB_BUS_ERROR | \
@@ -1356,6 +1363,13 @@
 		status = GMU_COLD_BOOT;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The GMU powers up with all of its HFI interrupts unmasked. The
+	 * replies to the boot messages are polled, so keep the interrupt line
+	 * quiet until the fault interrupt is set up below.
+	 */
+	gmu_write(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_MASK, ~0);
+
 	ret = a6xx_gmu_fw_start(gmu, status);
 	if (ret)
 		goto disable_irq;
